00:30:28 INFO [archeology_scan_6] Merged question: Would you generate a comprehensive report categorizing sites based on high-quality mesh standards? For each site, please include the site code, side name, total mesh count, high-fidelity mesh count and proportion (as a percentage), the average Mesh Complexity Ratio (MCR), average facet resolution parameters (in mm), average geometric accuracy measurements (Geom_Delta), and Mesh Quality.

The Mesh Quality Classification is defined as a three-tier system where sites are labeled 'Has High-Fidelity Meshes' if they contain at least one mesh meeting the high-fidelity criteria, 'Standard Mesh Quality' if they have meshes but none meeting high-fidelity criteria, and 'No Mesh Data' if they lack 3D mesh documentation entirely.

An individual mesh qualifies as high-fidelity only when ALL of the following conditions are met simultaneously:
1. Complexity Ratio (MCR) > 5.0, indicating structural complexity  
2. Facet Resolution (the mesh resolution measured in millimeters, Facet_Res_Mm) < 1.0 mm, ensuring fine geometric detail capture  
3. Geometric Accuracy (Geom_Delta) < 0.5 mm, ensuring precise geometric measurements  

Calculate the proportion of high-fidelity meshes as the count of high-fidelity meshes divided by the total mesh count at each site, expressed as a percentage. Present the data in descending order primarily by the high-fidelity mesh proportion (percentage), so sites with the highest percentage of high-quality meshes appear first. If two sites have the same high-fidelity percentage, break ties using secondary sorting criteria like the count of high-fidelity meshes or total mesh count.

For the average Mesh Complexity Ratio (MCR) per site, calculate the MCR individually for each mesh using the knowledge base formula: MCR = (Mesh Faces) / ((Mesh Vertices) × (Mesh Resolution (mm))²) × 10³, and then average those individual MCR values across all meshes at the site. This ensures each mesh's complexity is accurately captured based on its own faces, vertices, and resolution before deriving a meaningful average complexity ratio for the site.
