  raw: Find problematic shipments and show their shipment ID, the Time In Range Percentage (TIRP) that indicates how well the shipment maintained quality conditions during transit, and the total duration in minutes that the shipment experienced temperature excursions (Temperature Excursion Duration, TED). A shipment is considered problematic when the ratio of time without temperature excursions falls below 95%, meaning the shipment experienced temperature excursions for more than 5% of its total duration—that is, when (Time In Range Percentage) = (total transit time minus Temperature Excursion Duration) divided by total transit time times 100% is less than 95%. The Temperature Excursion Duration is a pre-calculated, aggregate metric stored in the monitoring data representing the total cumulative duration in minutes during which temperature readings fell outside the acceptable range throughout the entire shipment. It is not derived from intervals between consecutive readings but is the sum of all time periods when the temperature was out of specification. These metrics will help identify which shipments had the most significant quality control issues.

