For wash trading, severity is captured through the "wash_flag" field in our manipulation signals data, with three distinct severity levels contributing numeric values as follows: Low contributes 0.1 to the score, Medium contributes 0.5 to the score, and High contributes 1.0 to the score. These wash trading severity values are weighted at 10% of the overall suspicion calculation. Cases where wash_flag is not present or null do not contribute to this component.

The Composite Suspicion Score combines these behavioral signals with a weighting system where spoofing carries the highest weight, followed by front-running, then wash trading severity levels, and finally layering confirmation status. It also incorporates pattern recognition metrics to detect suspicious trading patterns and should be cross-referenced with compliance pattern recognition data to ensure we capture the full risk picture across both manipulation signals and regulatory compliance metrics.

The results should be sorted from highest to lowest Composite Suspicion Score, showing me the top 10 riskiest trades so I can prioritize which ones to review first.
