08:00:39 INFO   └─ [1;35muser answer:[0m [35mYou're right to question that result. The temperature coefficient is a negative value (around -0.389 for this panel model), which represents the fractional power loss per degree Celsius above the reference temperature of 25°C.

When the cell temperature is 36.1°C, the temperature deviation is (36.1 - 25) = 11.1°C. Multiplying by the absolute value of the temperature coefficient gives us the fractional loss: 11.1 × 0.389 ≈ 4.32%.

So the formula should be: `actual_power / (1 - loss_factor)`, where `loss_factor = ((cell_temp_c - 25) × ABS(tempcoeff))`. This gives us `(1 - 0.0432) ≈ 0.9568`, which produces a physically meaningful positive result.

The key is using the absolute value of the temperature coefficient and subtracting the loss factor from 1, which accounts for the power reduction due to elevated temperature and normalizes the actual power back to what it would be at the reference temperature of 25°C.[0m
